# Mosquitto ACL — development placeholder (task I01).
#
# The real per-vehicle file is GENERATED BY THE BACKEND in task B11 from the vehicle
# registry, together with the password file. Do not hand-maintain it there.
#
# Rules from docs/03-architecture/mqtt-topics.md (Access control):
#   - a vehicle user may PUBLISH only to its own .../veh/{vehicle_id}/gps and /status
#   - a vehicle user may SUBSCRIBE only to its own .../veh/{vehicle_id}/cmd
#   - the ingestor user subscribes to sc/v1/op/+/veh/+/gps and .../status
#
# IMPORTANT (Mosquitto 2 behaviour): rules before the first `user`/`pattern` line apply
# to anonymous clients. Dev runs anonymous, so the open rule below is what makes
# `make up` usable. Removing it without also disabling anonymous access will silently
# deny every publish.

# --- development: anonymous clients may use the whole versioned topic tree ---
topic readwrite sc/v1/#

# --- development: authenticated clients too -----------------------------------
# A client that sends a username is NOT anonymous, so the rule above does not cover it,
# and Mosquitto denies its publishes silently at QoS 0. The simulator (M04) and the
# driver app both authenticate as their vehicle, so without this `make up` accepts the
# connection and then drops every ping with no error anywhere - which reads exactly like
# "MQTT is broken". Production replaces this whole file with the generated per-vehicle
# one (B11), where each user may write only to its own topic.
pattern readwrite sc/v1/#

# --- template for the generated production file (task B11) -------------------
# Per-vehicle credentials, one block per vehicle, username = vehicle UUID:
#
#   user 6f1d2c4e-0a1b-4c3d-8e9f-000000000001
#   topic write sc/v1/op/%c/veh/6f1d2c4e-0a1b-4c3d-8e9f-000000000001/gps
#   topic write sc/v1/op/%c/veh/6f1d2c4e-0a1b-4c3d-8e9f-000000000001/status
#   topic read  sc/v1/op/%c/veh/6f1d2c4e-0a1b-4c3d-8e9f-000000000001/cmd
#
# Equivalent pattern form, where %u is the connected username (the vehicle UUID):
#
#   pattern write sc/v1/op/+/veh/%u/gps
#   pattern write sc/v1/op/+/veh/%u/status
#   pattern read  sc/v1/op/+/veh/%u/cmd
#
# The ingestor is a single privileged consumer:
#
#   user ingestor
#   topic read sc/v1/op/+/veh/+/gps
#   topic read sc/v1/op/+/veh/+/status
#   topic write sc/v1/op/+/veh/+/cmd
